WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.580
CI/CD systems often hold powerful credentials because they publish packages, sign binaries, access private dependencies, deploy software, update release records, or communicate with cloud services.

2
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:23.080
Those credentials may include package registry tokens, deployment credentials, code-signing keys, certificate material, SSH keys, API tokens, and service credentials.

3
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:28.140
The pipeline should treat that access as production capability, not merely build convenience.

4
00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.980
Least privilege is the starting point.

5
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:36.360
A job that builds pull requests should not automatically receive production deployment credentials.

6
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.580
A test job should not inherit signing material.

7
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.440
A packaging job should receive only the access it needs for its specific task and environment.

8
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.820
Where available, short-lived credentials and environment-scoped access reduce long-term exposure.

9
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.760
Access should be reviewed when job responsibilities change.

10
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.240
Logs and artifacts need special attention.

11
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:10.500
Secrets can appear through command echoes, failed scripts, verbose package managers, test output, crash reports, cache directories, temporary files, and bundled build artifacts.

12
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.040
Masking can help, but masking is not a guarantee.

13
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:20.700
Design jobs so sensitive values are not printed, copied, cached, or packaged in the first place.

14
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.160
Review failure paths because diagnostic output often reveals more than success output.

15
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.960
Signing keys and certificate material need clear policy.

16
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:41.340
Define who can trigger signing, which artifacts may be signed, how approvals work, how keys are protected, how rotation and revocation are handled, and what evidence is retained.

17
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.720
Separating jobs that need secrets from jobs that do not is one of the most practical ways to keep the pipeline safer and easier to review.

18
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:56.180
It also reduces the number of logs, caches, and scripts that must be treated as highly sensitive during audits and incident review.

19
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.200
Every exception should have an owner.
